WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.905
Level 0 represents the physical process itself.

00:00:02.905 --> 00:00:06.912
It includes the equipment and conditions being measured or influenced:

00:00:06.912 --> 00:00:10.918
material moving through a line, water in a treatment stage,

00:00:10.918 --> 00:00:14.925
power flowing through a substation, or temperature inside a vessel.

00:00:14.925 --> 00:00:18.131
This is where digital decisions become physical outcomes.

00:00:18.131 --> 00:00:22.939
Sensors and instrumentation report process conditions such as pressure, flow, speed, position,

00:00:22.939 --> 00:00:23.740
and temperature.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:25.343
Actuators create physical change.

00:00:25.343 --> 00:00:29.350
They open valves, start motors, move mechanisms, or operate breakers.

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:34.158
A false measurement or incorrect actuation can affect product quality, equipment, safety,

00:00:34.158 --> 00:00:35.360
and the environment.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:37.364
Level 1 provides basic control.

00:00:37.364 --> 00:00:40.970
Programmable logic controllers, remote terminal units, distributed control components,

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:45.377
and other controllers read inputs, apply control logic, and update outputs.

00:00:45.377 --> 00:00:49.384
Many perform this work continuously and within strict timing requirements.

00:00:49.384 --> 00:00:52.990
Their configuration, logic, and communication relationships are operationally sensitive.

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:56.596
Security at these levels must respect safety and reliability.

00:00:56.596 --> 00:00:58.600
Aggressive scanning, untested endpoint software,

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:02.206
or an unexpected restart can interfere with deterministic control.

00:01:02.206 --> 00:01:06.213
Protection often relies on physical safeguards, restricted access, network isolation,

00:01:06.213 --> 00:01:09.018
authenticated engineering workflows, configuration backups, change control,

00:01:09.018 --> 00:01:11.422
and monitoring at nearby network points.

00:01:11.422 --> 00:01:16.230
Level 0 and Level 1 also remind analysts that a device's consequence

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:19.836
cannot be inferred from its size or operating system.

00:01:19.836 --> 00:01:23.042
A small controller may influence a critical process.

00:01:23.042 --> 00:01:26.247
Risk assessment should identify what the device controls,

00:01:26.247 --> 00:01:31.055
what independent safety functions exist, how loss of communication affects the process,

00:01:31.055 --> 00:01:33.860
and how trusted operation can be restored.
